diagnosis_text:
After a thorough examination of every relevant layer — physical interfaces, EIGRP adjacency state and topology tables, OSPF adjacency state and LSA database, FRR daemon health, startup scripts, background processes, traffic-control rules, and live data-plane reachability — no fault is present in this network.

r12 (120.1.1.1, PC12's gateway) is fully operational: eth1 is UP with zero errors, it maintains a stable EIGRP adjacency with r11 (hold timer 10–12 s, no flap events in the FRR log since startup), and its static default route toward r11 is installed in the kernel. r11's OSPF adjacency to r1 is Full/DR with a 4m55s uptime and default hello/dead timers (10/40 s), and r11's routing table is correctly populated with inter-area OSPF routes from r1 covering all internet-facing prefixes. A live 5-packet ping from PC12 through r12 and r11 all the way to r1 completed with 0% loss.

The user's assertion that "the port my machine hangs off that box is obviously failing" is not supported by any evidence: r12.eth1 has no error counters, no drop counters, no carrier-loss events, and no EIGRP adjacency flaps. The "few seconds of cutting out at random" pattern is not reproduced by any observable mechanism in the infrastructure — no route flapping, no daemon restarts, no scheduled disruption scripts.

The most likely explanation is that the user is experiencing a non-infrastructure issue (e.g., an application-layer timeout, a client-side ARP or DNS cache quirk, or transient packet loss on the physical host bridging the GG segment that is not visible from inside the containers). The network infrastructure itself is healthy and requires no corrective action.
